@* PasswordField's Overview, written by hand, after TextField's: show and hide, a new password's
   requirements, the field after a failed post, then the app's text. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>A password field hides what's typed as dots, with a button that shows it. It's a native password input, so it posts with the form, <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">with JavaScript off</Anchor> too, and password managers fill it. Its label, hint and limits come from the model, as on a <Anchor Href="docs/components/text-field">TextField</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Show and hide</Heading>
<TextBlock>Once IzzyBlazor's script runs, the field gets a button that shows and hides the password. To leave the button out, set <Code>Reveal="false"</Code>. While the user types with Caps Lock on, "Caps Lock is on." shows under the field.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="password-field-reveal" />

<Heading Level="2">A new password's requirements</Heading>
<TextBlock>A field for a new password can list the rules a password must meet, and check off each one as the user types. Set <Code>Requirements</Code> to the rules your server checks.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="password-field-new-password" />
<TextBlock>The browser doesn't post the form until the password meets all of them, and the server checks them again, so a form post with JavaScript off gets the same check. <Code>AutoComplete</Code> becomes <Code>new-password</Code>, and Safari suggests passwords that meet the rules.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock><Code>PasswordRules</Code> uses the names of ASP.NET Core Identity's <Code>PasswordOptions</Code>, and counts characters as Identity does: <Code>RequiredLength</Code>, <Code>RequireDigit</Code>, <Code>RequireLowercase</Code>, <Code>RequireUppercase</Code>, <Code>RequireNonAlphanumeric</Code> and <Code>RequiredUniqueChars</Code>. Its rules are off until set, where Identity's defaults are on, so copy each of Identity's values across for the list and the server to agree.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock><Anchor Href="https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-4/sp800-63b.html">NIST SP 800-63B</Anchor> asks for a length of at least 15 characters, or 8 where a second factor is also needed, and says not to impose other composition rules, such as "a number". The other rules are here because many existing apps have them.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>NIST also asks for a check against commonly used and breached passwords. Your server makes that check when it saves the password, such as with <Anchor Href="https://haveibeenpwned.com/API/v3#PwnedPasswords">Pwned Passwords</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">After a failed post</Heading>
<TextBlock>On a static page, the field is empty after a post that fails: the page doesn't write the password back into its HTML, so the user types it again. On an interactive page, nothing reloads and it stays.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="password-field-sign-in" />

<Heading Level="2">Labels and localization</Heading>
<TextBlock>The show and hide button has a name for screen readers. To set it for the whole app, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/text">Text and other languages</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Sets</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><Code>ShowPasswordLabel</Code></td><td>The name of the button that shows and hides a password</td><td>"Show password"</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</Prose>
<TextBlock>The Caps Lock warning and the requirements come from the app's text:</TextBlock>
<Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>IzzyText</th><th>Sets</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><Code>CapsLockOn</Code></td><td>The warning under the field</td><td>"Caps Lock is on."</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>PasswordLength</Code>, <Code>PasswordUnique</Code></td><td>The length and different-characters rules; <Code>{0}</Code> is the number</td><td>"At least {0} characters", "At least {0} different characters"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>PasswordLowercase</Code>, <Code>PasswordUppercase</Code>, <Code>PasswordDigit</Code>, <Code>PasswordSymbol</Code></td><td>The other rules</td><td>"A lowercase letter", "An uppercase letter", "A number", "A symbol, such as ! or #"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>RuleMet</Code>, <Code>RuleNotMet</Code></td><td>What screen readers hear after each rule</td><td>"met", "not yet"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>PasswordRulesMet</Code></td><td>Announced once all the rules are met</td><td>"All requirements met."</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>PasswordRulesUnmet</Code></td><td>The error for a password that misses one; <Code>{0}</Code> is the label</td><td>"The {0} field doesn't meet every requirement."</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</Prose>

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<Heading Level="2">See also</Heading>
<List Gap="2">
    <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/text-field">TextField</Anchor>: one line of text, with icons, add-ons and masks.</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/pin-field">PinField</Anchor>: a one-time code, one box per character.</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/form">Form</Anchor>: posts on static pages and validates on interactive ones.</ListItem>
    <TroubleshootingLinks Component="PasswordField" />
</List>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        @* Signing in. *@
        <PasswordField @bind-Value="Model.Password" AutoComplete="current-password" />

        @* A new password, with the rules the server checks. *@
        <PasswordField @bind-Value="Model.NewPassword"
                       Requirements="new PasswordRules { RequiredLength = 15 }" />
        """;
}
